<article class="env-remark" id="04BZ">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/04BZ">Remark <span data-tag="04BZ">37.9.11</span>.</a> Lemma <a href="/tag/04BY" data-tag="04BY">37.9.10</a> can be improved in the following way. Suppose that we have commutative diagrams as in Lemma <a href="/tag/04BY" data-tag="04BY">37.9.10</a> but we do not assume that $X_2 \to X_1$ and $S_2 \to S_1$ are étale. Next, suppose we have $\theta _1 : a_1^*\Omega _{X_1/S_1} \to \mathcal{C}_{T_1/T'_1}$ and $\theta _2 : a_2^*\Omega _{X_2/S_2} \to \mathcal{C}_{T_2/T'_2}$ such that </p>
  <div class="equation">
  \[  \xymatrix{ f_*\mathcal{O}_{X_2} \ar[rr]_{f_*D_2} &  &  f_*a_{2, *}\mathcal{C}_{T_2/T_2'} \\  \mathcal{O}_{X_1} \ar[rr]^{D_1} \ar[u]^{f^\sharp } &  &  a_{1, *}\mathcal{C}_{T_1/T_1'} \ar[u]_{\text{induced by }(h')^\sharp } }  \]
</div>
  <p> is commutative where $D_ i$ corresponds to $\theta _ i$ as in Equation (<a href="/tag/04BV" data-tag="04BV">37.9.1.1</a>). Then we have the conclusion of Lemma <a href="/tag/04BY" data-tag="04BY">37.9.10</a>. The importance of the condition that both $X_2 \to X_1$ and $S_2 \to S_1$ are étale is that it allows us to construct a $\theta _2$ from $\theta _1$. </p>
</article>